    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d672">

                <pb facs="0652" n="648" xml:id="pg648"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">693.002/487: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-02-04T18:00:00+08:00">February 4, 1938—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received February 4—3:45 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>207. Reference my No. 140, January 26, 1 p.m. Inspector General now writes me
                    that the question being urgent and the Ministry of Finance having pressed him
                    for further information wishes to know with as little delay as possible, (1),
                    the view of the American Government vis-à-vis suggested reintroduction of 1931
                    tariff with some modifications and (2), whether there are any articles not
                    including those dealt with by the Peiping <hi rendition="#italic">de facto</hi>
                    government on which the American authorities and nationals wish to see rates
                    different from those given in the 1931 tariff.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Hankow and Peiping.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
